{
  "WHAT_THIS_IS": "The headline metric (section 8.4's plain loss barrier) on MANY held-out windows.\n\nWHY. Both pilot write-ups computed the barrier on ONE committed 1024-token\npassage. The barrier is the study's headline metric, so its window-to-window\nvariability -- not the endpoint loss's -- is what actually sets the seed count.\nNobody has measured it. This measures it.\n\nWHAT IS AND IS NOT CHANGED. The metric is unchanged: same alpha grid, same\n`excess(alpha) = L(alpha) - chord` arithmetic, same fp64 cross entropy. The only\nchange is the evaluation batch, from one passage to W held-out windows. That is\nthe change docs/measurements/10-metrics.md says to make \"once against trained\ncheckpoints\", and the slice it uses is the one cross-module obligation 1\nreserved.\n\nTWO NUMBERS COME OUT, and they answer different questions.\n\n  widened-batch barrier -- L(alpha) is the mean over all W windows, then the\n      barrier arithmetic runs on that curve. This is what the study's headline\n      number would be if the batch were widened. One number per pair.\n\n  per-window barriers -- the barrier arithmetic run separately on each window's\n      own curve, giving W barriers per pair. Their spread is how much the\n      single-passage choice was worth, and it is the thing the pilots could not\n      see.\n\nTHE GATE. Before any of that, the same loop is run on the committed\nbursts/context.txt batch for one pair and compared against the committed number\nin docs/measurements/2026-08-06-pilot-v2-barriers/. If this file's loop does not\nreproduce a measurement already on record, none of its other numbers are worth\nreading.",
